The world behind the terminal
Before the patterns, Yuri set the stakes. A “payment” isn’t one app. It’s a mobile sales app, a point-of-sale app, a payment terminal running on a locked-down OS, payment gateways, terminal management systems, merchant platforms, and, underneath it all, processors, banks, and regulators. Each country carries thousands of pages of specifications and roughly 1,000 scenarios that must be met before anything reaches production.
Some of those terminals have to keep working offline for up to 40 days, by regulation, because in the country they operate in, a payment terminal is critical infrastructure on the same level as a gas station or a pharmacy. Payment teams also have to satisfy contradictory rules from different regulators governing the same shared infrastructure: one country requires a behavior, another forbids it, and both must be enforced in the same codebase. That’s the environment the type system has to hold up in. Not a demo. Production, worldwide, for decades, not for a year.
